- F-380 **A numeral at the end of a block, or in a tuple, converts where it stands on the compiled path.** At a declared type (a return type, a typed binding, a parameter, an `if`'s other branch) the compiled checker converts a numeral that ends a block after a local declaration, and each numeral element of a tuple, by the type's own coercion of the numeral itself, so the code generator folds it into a constant; such a numeral outside the type's range is refused statically, as at a block's end without a declaration. At a place that fixes no type, a block after a local declaration keeps its numeral's own type, and a later conversion of a variable bound to that block dies (row 1060). Tests: `compiler_tests/NumeralEndsBlockAfterDeclarationCompiled`, `BlockEndsInTupleOfNumeralCompiled`, `TupleBindingIfBranchesCompiled`, `NumeralJoinBeyondRange.fss` with `XXXNumeralJoinBeyondRange.test`; `XXXNumeralEndsUntypedBlockCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-numeral-block-end/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why", which names the checker's members; `compile-ladder/rung-numeral-block-end/SKEPTIC.md`, "Findings and fixes".
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-numeral-block-end/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-numeral-block-end/SKEPTIC.md, "Findings and fixes"; commit: 00dd16a89; note: the pass at climb batch 31b's landing: past 600 characters; the checker's members `numeralResult` and `joinInto` (`Functionals.scala`) and `NumeralConversion.outOfRangeAt` (`CoercionOracle.scala`) left to the rung's report and its skeptic's, which name them; in the fix round, "the variable" named as the variable bound to that block, for a cold reader

- F-257 **Under walk, a pair of declarations that the load check refuses after it reads the pair on its declared domains is refused in the name of the rule that the pair breaks.** The load check reads a pair on its declared domains where its parameter-by-parameter check refuses the pair and both declarations have static parameters of their own, or one has them and the other is a plain declaration or a generic trait's functional method that declares none of its own (F-252, F-27, F-318), and where that check finds a plain declaration inside an own generic's declared domain excluding at object types alone (F-375). It reads a pair that the check orders only for the No Duplicates Rule (F-274). The messages open "fails the Return Type Rule:" and "fails the Meet Rule:", and the No Duplicates Rule's is walk's "fails because their parameter lists have the same types"; the report gives them whole. The first two contain the words "not found" because walk's reading is narrower than the rules: rows 539, 779 and 1058 hold valid pairs that walk refuses. A pair that is not read on its declared domains keeps the parameter check's message, such as "with generic type, at least one pair of parameters must have excluding types". Such a pair has a varargs parameter, or parameter lists of different lengths, or raises an error while its instances are read. Or it is of none of the kinds above, as two functional methods of generic traits, or it is the pair of instances that an explicit instantiation such as `f[\A, B\]` makes of two generic declarations of one name (`XXXGenericOverload2`). The message does not decide which pairs load. Gated by `tests/GenericReturnRuleInvariantWalk`, `GenericReturnRuleOuterParamWalk` and `GenericBesidePlainReturnRuleWalk` (Return Type), `GenericOverloadInstancesApartWalk`, `GenericBoundsNoMeetWalk` and `GenericBesidePlainNoMeetWalk` (Meet), and `NoDuplicatesRuleWalk` and `NoDuplicatesRuleSizeWalk` (No Duplicates). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-trait-method-bounds/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 3.
  from: explorations/reviews/batch-31b-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; commit: 00dd16a89; note: row 550 is fixed by rung O of climb batch 31b (`f6e24650e`); row 1058, filed by its skeptic, holds a valid pair that walk refuses with 'fails the Return Type Rule'; the batch file's list for the record-landing names the clause (`CLIMB-BATCH-31.md:1003`)

- F-240 **The checker's count stage takes about 85 s and its distance stage 150 s at the last landed gate, climb batch 31b's.** At climb batch 15b's gate they took 325 s and 1,075 s; the solver's memos (F-235) make most of the difference, not load. Its rise from about 90 s at climb batch 21b's gate is the `FortressLibrary` component's check now run to its end, past its cleared type errors into the export check, not load: rung runs that stopped at those errors took 91 s and 99 s, runs past them 149 s and 151 s, one run each. Since climb batch 21b the checker checks every symbolic operator (F-281), which costs the count stage about 15 to 25 % by its rung's timings of its base and its head in one session. From climb batch 22's gate to climb batch 26's it stayed between about 150 s and 180 s. The count table records no seconds, so its time is the file times of the gate's log; the distance stage (`FortressLibrary` 96 s), over fourteen components, ran at a start load of 0.31, then beside the first minutes of `testFast`, so its time is one under that suite's load, against 143 s (86 s) at 0.08 at climb batch 31's. Both stages about halved at climb batch 27's gate, and no run measured why: each gate ran each stage once, and neither the emptied `Number` (F-324) nor the distance's check stopped at `Char` (F-54) is shown to be the cause. Load can lengthen a stage more than twofold: climb batch 16's merged code took 217 s for the distance stage at its gate, at a load average of 3.3, and nearly the same code took 574 s at 4.6 to 5.3. So a time compares only with one taken under a like load. Source: the file times of the gate's log and `compile-ladder/climb-batch-31b/gate/distance.txt`. Detail: `reviews/batch-31-review.md`, section 4; `reviews/batch-32-review.md`, section 4; `reviews/batch-27-review.md`, section 2; `reviews/batch-26-review.md`, section 2; `reviews/batch-25-review.md`, section 2; `reviews/batch-24-review.md`, section 2; `reviews/batch-23-review.md`, section 2; `reviews/batch-22c-review.md`, section 2; `reviews/batch-22-review.md`, section 2; `reviews/batch-21b-review.md`, section 2; `reviews/batch-17-review.md`, section 1; `reviews/batch-16-review.md`, section 2.
  from: explorations/reviews/batch-31b-review.md, "4. The gate, the count and the distance"; commit: 00dd16a89; note: the clause that explained a fall of the count stage since climb batch 31's gate cut, since the gate of climb batch 31b shows a rise, about 85 s against about 75 s, as the commit stage noted; every figure stays the commit stage's

- F-285 **The interpreter's library writes the static argument of every clause form whose big operator's bound names its parameter, and of every list comprehension, in the twelve components the distance checks; `__bigOperator2`'s fused arm casts its result to `O0`.** It follows P-78: `BIG MIN[\ZZ32\]` in `String`'s `upto` and `beyond` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4665`, `:4671`), and `<|[\E\] self[i] | i <- r |>` in `List`'s range subscript (`Library/List.fss:151`), beside `SUM[\T\]` at `:2804`, `:3190`, `:3195`, `SUM[\ZZ32\]` at `:4849`, `:4866`, and the team's `transpose` (`List.fss:504`). The clause forms left unwritten are of plain-bounded big operators (`BIG ||`, `BIG AND`, `BIG OR`), which the checker takes at their bound, or of `BIG LEXICO`, which has no static parameter. The fused arm (`:1670`) uses the library's cast device for an arm whose type the `typecase` cannot show (F-264), as `__builtinFactory1`'s `N[\0\]` arm does (`:2824`). So the distance holds no site of an unwritten argument or of the fused arm. The checker now infers such an unwritten argument itself (F-364); the library's sites stay written. Under walk, the written comprehension makes `List`'s strided subscript answer a `List[\E\]`; `PureList`'s, at `Library/PureList.fss:73`, answers a list of objects at their elements' run-time types, and a list of `ZZ32` or `String` at its element type (row 824). Tests: the distance stage (`explorations/coordinator/tools/distance/run.sh`, its typecheck kind 0); `tests/WrittenReductionArgumentsWalk`; `tests/NestedReductionFusion`; `tests/PureListStridedSubscriptWalk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-body-arguments/REPORT.md`, sections 3, 5, 7 and 9.
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-number-objects/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 00dd16a89; note: rung O of climb batch 31b moved seven more citations of `Library/FortressLibrary.fss` by 84 lines; each re-anchored by its content at 00dd16a89 (the fused arm's `cast[\O0\]`, the four `SUM`s, the `N[\0\]` arm); the merged-diff review had moved `:4581` and `:4587` only

- F-75 **The compiled checker checks the Return Type Rule over every instance, save those whose domain cannot meet an object type, and a positional rule: when two generic declarations in the more-specific relation declare as many static parameters of their own, those parameters agree in kind by position, and the more specific return type is a subtype of the other once the other's parameters are read as its own by position.** The equal-count scope and the comparison of kinds are the rung's choices. It builds P-45. The Return Type Rule leaves out the instances of the less specific declaration whose domain cannot meet the other's only where the less specific declaration has a type parameter as a whole parameter type and the more specific one has an object type there; a worker chose that width, and the curator has not decided it. The type group's 2011 paper (`Papers/Types/`) sets aside every instance whose domain is `Bottom`. So the rule accepts `ident[\T\](x: T): T` beside `ident(x: Circle): Circle`, `Circle` an object, and the pair runs (row 542, fixed; F-376). At a tuple of object types the rule still compares the instances that cannot meet, and wrongly refuses `ident[\T\](x: T): T` beside `ident(x: (Circle, Circle)): (Circle, Circle)` (row 1048). In the interpreter's library, with the built-in number types merged with their objects (P-178, F-379), the two rules refuse no pair: the distance holds no return-type or overloading error (F-53). Gated by `compiler_tests/OverloadGeneric*`, `OverloadBoundNamesForced` and `OverloadReturnObjectDomain`, which accept; `XXXOverloadReturnEveryInstance`, `XXXOverloadReturnObjectDomainOtherType`, `XXXOverloadGenericObjectDomainByPosition` and `XXXOverloadPermutedStaticParams`, which pin the two rules' right refusals; and `XXXOverloadReturnTupleDomain`, which pins row 1048's. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-return-type-rule/decision-record.md` sections 1 and 2; `compile-ladder/rung-return-rule-object/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: explorations/reviews/batch-31b-review.md, "4. The gate, the count and the distance"; commit: 00dd16a89; note: the clause that the merge of the number types brings no refusal, bounded at climb batch 31's landing as read and not run, is now shown by the distance on the merged library at climb batch 31b's gate, 0 with no return-type or overloading error; folded into the sentence on the distance

- F-139 **`RR32` is a sibling of `RR64` under `Number`, and `RR64` converts from it.** Nothing converts into `RR32`. Under `walk` two `RR32`s answer an `RR32`, and an `RR32` with an `RR64`, a float numeral or a `ZZ32` answers an `RR64`. A typed binding converts an `RR32`, but a declared return does not (row 387). On the compiled path `=` at the prelude's `RR32` overflows the stack (row 528), and two `RR32`s answer an `RR64` (row 529). Gated by `tests/RR32SiblingRungV.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-rr32-sibling/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 11.
  from: explorations/fortress-gap-ledger.md, "7. Numerals, floats and comparisons"; commit: 00dd16a89; note: rung E of climb batch 31b rewrote row 528: the prelude's RR32 takes its = from the trait Equality, which calls ===, which calls =, so the call overflows the stack; the row's earlier mechanism, no `=`, had gone stale

- F-140 **A size beyond `NN32` or `ZZ32` is refused by the compiled checker when it is a literal or folded from literals, and by walk when it binds one; walk reads a size exactly.** It builds P-40. The checker refuses such a size outside its kind: 0 to 4294967295 for `nat`, and `ZZ32`'s range for `int`. Walk refuses it at run time, where it binds the size to a static parameter. Walk reads and computes a size exactly, with no wrap: it reads a size used as a value as a `ZZ32` up to 2^31-1 and as a `ZZ64` above. The checker types such a value `ZZ32` (P-173, F-342). The compiled class loader checks no size that it computes at instantiation: `next[\nat k\]()`, which builds a `Box[\k + 1\]`, answers 4294967296 at `k = 4294967295` (gap-ledger row 675; F-222). Gated by `tests/NatBigSizeWalk` for walk's reading. The refusals are gated by `compiler_tests/XXXNatRangeChecker`, `XXXNatArithRangeChecker` and `NatRtBigSize`, and by `tests/XXXNatBeyondNN32Walk`, `XXXIntBeyondZZ32Walk`, `XXXIntBelowZZ32Walk`, `XXXNatArithProductWalk` and `XXXNatArithSumWalk`; each `XXX` test passes because its program is refused. Open under walk: an array of a static size from 2^31 to 2^32-1 stops at its store's factory, 'Closure/Constructor for makeJavaArrayStore parameter 1 (n:ZZ32) got type ZZ64', where no catch sees it (row 527). Also open (row 962, contested): a `nat` or `int` size of 2^31 or more read as a value raises a `java.lang.Error` on the compiled path that no `catch` sees, and is a `ZZ64` under walk, where P-173 makes it a `ZZ32` and no decision says where such a size is refused; under walk a `ZZ32`-declared return passes such a size on as a `ZZ64`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-size-range/REPORT.md` sections 6 and 12.
  from: explorations/reviews/batch-31b-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; commit: 00dd16a89; note: row 527's message reads 'got type ZZ64' since `12292add28` of 2026-11-09; the entry kept the earlier 'got type Long'; the post-batch review of climb batch 31b found it

- P-68 **On the float types, `floor` and `ceiling` return the same float type, while the brackets ⌊x⌋ and ⌈x⌉, `round` and `truncate` return `ZZ`, the unbounded integer.** So `floor(2.5)` is the `RR64` value 2.0, and ⌊2.5⌋ is to be the `ZZ` value 2. Row 330: one library rung after the switch-over, which first counts the interpreter tests whose output changes, then makes the brackets, `round` and `truncate` return `ZZ` in each of the library's declarations of them; today `truncate` is `ZZ64` on both paths, the brackets are `ZZ64` under walk and `RR64` compiled, and `round` is `ZZ64`.
  from: explorations/reviews/batch-31b-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; commit: 00dd16a89; note: the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 for a sentence that the tree made false; rung O of climb batch 31b removed `trait RR64` and its `round(self): ZZ`, and the one `round` is `round(self):ZZ64` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:548`); the clause naming the trait's `ZZ` cut; the batch file's list for the record-landing names it (`CLIMB-BATCH-31.md:1006`)

- P-166 **`ZZ`'s and `QQ`'s values are value objects, and the specification's list of reference objects stops naming arbitrary-precision numbers.** The library declares `value object ZZ` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:1170`), so every `ZZ` value is a value object, and `QQ`'s object `Ratio` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:719`) is a `value object` too. By the specification's rule two value objects of one type are strictly equivalent when their fields are, so two `ZZ` values built apart are `SEQV` when their integers are equal, `SEQV` converting each into its bits and comparing those, and two equal rationals built apart are `SEQV` in the same way. The specification's list in `Specification/basic/objects.tex` ("Reference objects include arbitrary-precision numbers") is revised to name them among the value objects, in the revision form (P-96) with one Appendix I entry. Why: the library's `value` declaration is the deliberate word, and where it is, the curator's rule brings the specification's example in line with the code. Detail: `reviews/any-carrier-2026-11-06.md`, section 2.4, point 11, which found the split.
  from: explorations/reviews/batch-31b-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; commit: 00dd16a89; note: the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 for a sentence that the tree made false; rung O of climb batch 31b deleted the declaration value object BigNum extends ZZ from the component FortressBuiltin and made ZZ itself a value object; Ratio moved from line 649 to line 719

- P-162 **The 30 native bindings that no bare helper reaches: those over `AnyIntegral` or `Number` become public converting declarations around bare bindings at the prelude's narrow headers, those over carried types get new Java helpers, and the three over `Any` leave glue by a way of their own.** The 20 over `AnyIntegral` or `Number`, the shifts and powers of the five integer types, `RR64`'s power, `random`, `atan2` and `printThreadInfo`, keep their public headers; each converts its argument in Fortress and calls a public bare `builtinPrimitive("…")` binding (P-58) at the narrow header that the compiler's prelude declares, the team's pair, with the bare half private where a stage refuses a pair. Such a pair keeps P-58's form, and climb batch 26's five pairs over `FlatString` (F-299) are this pattern. The 6 over carried types with no fitting helper, `RR64`'s floor and ceiling brackets, the mixed powers of `RR64` and `RR32`, and `FlatString`'s `=` and `flatConcat(self, b: Char)`, each get a one-line helper. `ZZ32`'s `big` names `makeZZfromZZ32`. `Object`'s `asString` and `ilkName` and `SEQV` over `Any` leave their glue classes (F-32) by P-167's way, and P-168 settles what `SEQV` means, which both paths need since walk compares numbers and tuples by value and the compiled helper compares references. A shift count keeps any integer type, a fixed-width shift never goes through `ZZ` (P-69), and each integer power answers its base's type (P-153). Why: the team moved this conversion into Fortress in 2008 for the defect that group 2 still shows at a rational, and the prelude of 2011 and 2012 converts around narrow helpers and never binds a native at `AnyIntegral` or `Number`; the model's power calls, all at a `ZZ32` exponent, then reach a bare binding with no extra call. For the six, a helper is one call that the JIT inlines whole on the compiled path, where a Fortress composition is two or three calls and a temporary box (F-301, F-302). Detail: `reviews/hard-set-natives-2026-11-06.md`, sections 1, 6, 7 and 8.
  from: explorations/reviews/batch-31b-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; commit: 00dd16a89; note: the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 for a sentence that the tree made false; rung O of climb batch 31b merged `Float` into `RR64`, whose object holds `Float`'s bindings (F-303, F-323); a respelling, no change of meaning

- P-165 **`asFloat` and the `=` built on it leave `trait Number`; each built-in number type keeps its own `asFloat`.** `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `NN32`, `NN64`, `ZZ`, `QQ`, `RR32` and `RR64` each declare `asFloat`, so the written conversion `asFloat(x)` into `RR64` is unchanged at every concrete type; the numeral types declare none, since no numeral value exists when a program runs (P-174). Two numbers of different types compare by `=` only where a coercion carries one into the other (P-74); otherwise the program writes the conversion, `asFloat(x) = y`. The vector norm takes a bound that promises a float; its form is the default of a fork on his review list: the norm at each of the eight number types, each by its own `asFloat` (F-324). `RR64`'s `^` at a `Number` exponent, `random`, `atan2` and `printThreadInfo` are declared at `RR64`, and coercion carries `ZZ32`, `RR32` and numerals to them. The specification's sentence follows (`Specification/basic-lib/numbers.tex:58-75`). P-74's rule, an exact conversion implicit and a lossy one written, stands. Why: the team's compiler prelude declares `Number` with nothing in it (`LibraryBuiltin/CompilerBuiltin.fss:509`), and the specification does not ground the method: its sentence that names it on `Number` is the revival's revision. The curator's rule for such a case: an early idea of the interpreter's library goes where the specification does not ground it and the later prelude eliminated it. With `Number` open (P-160), an abstract `asFloat` would force a program's own number, such as a complex number, to invent a float, and `Number`'s `=` would compare two different complex numbers by it.
  from: explorations/reviews/batch-31b-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; commit: 00dd16a89; note: the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 for a sentence that the tree made false; rung O of climb batch 31b merged `Float` into `RR64` (F-323); a respelling, no change of meaning

- P-163 **`Char`'s `asExprString` writes, for every code point, a text that reads back as the same character: the compiled helper's form, completed.** The helper `nativeHelpers/simpleChar.charToExprString` escapes `"` and `\` as `'\"'` and `'\\'`, writes a printable character as itself with its code, the redundant form `'é 00e9'` that Steele built in 2011, and writes the code alone where the parser cannot read the character: the controls, U+201C, U+201D, U+2028, U+2029 and the surrogates. Walk's binding (`ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fss:409`) names that helper, as P-58 asks of every native binding, so both paths write the same text. The parser tries its longer choices of hexadecimal digits first (`parser/Literal.rats:460-466`), so that a six-digit code reads back. The specification's list of character-literal forms (`Specification/basic/lexical-structure.tex:860-891`) is revised in the revision form (P-96) to name the redundant form, which the parser has read since 2011, with an Appendix I entry. Why: the library's contract, the team's comment "will equal self", holds at every code point only with the code alone or with this form; this form is the team's later word, Steele's helper and the parser rule that he wrote with it in one commit, with his own two escape cases reached; and the peers write a printable character as itself, where the code alone hides it. The compiled path's text changes at about 2K code points and walk's at every code point outside printable ASCII, and no library code, suite test or model line calls a `Char`'s `asExprString`. Detail: `reviews/char-asexprstring-2026-11-06.md`, sections 1, 6 (d), 7 and 8.
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-number-objects/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 00dd16a89; note: the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 for a sentence that the tree made false; rung O of climb batch 31b deleted 186 lines of ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fss, and the binding of simpleChar.charToExprString moved from line 594 to line 409, checked at 00dd16a89
